 "tags": "dolphin bottlenose sea ocean swim mammal reef friendly",
 "swim": true,
 "alt": 170.0,
 "review": {
  "score": 7.0,
  "round": 3,
  "problems": [
   "It reads as a bottlenose dolphin at a glance. The silhouette has a beak, a curved dorsal fin, a pale belly and a grey back, so nothing reads wrong.",
   "The tail flukes are small and spindly for a 480-long body. The peduncle (tail stalk) narrows to about 30 units, and there is a visible seam where the tail part meets the body at x\u2248-140/-170.",
   "The idle, walking and walking-later frames are almost identical. The tail and body barely move, so the swim cycle is hard to see.",
   "The beak and mouth line are faint. The dark mouth curve and the small pale jaw wedge look like a slight overbite rather than a smile, so the face is only mildly friendly.",
   "In the scale frame the dolphin is semi-transparent and hovers at the person's waist. That is acceptable for a swimmer, but its length is only about 1.1x the person's height. It should be about 1.4x."
  ]
 },
 "for": "a friendly bottlenose dolphin swimming: beak snout, curved dorsal fin, tail flukes, grey back and pale belly",
 "source": "minted",
 "created": "2026-11-02",
 "sprite": {
  "w": 480.0,
  "h": 220.0,
  "ax": 0.5,
  "ay": 0.97,
  "src": "mg/library/mint/sprites/dolphin.png"
 },
 "sprite_review": {
  "score": 8.0,
  "problems": [
   "The tail flukes are small and thin compared with the body. At thumbnail size the tail reads more like a fish tail than a broad dolphin fluke.",
   "The dolphin is a little short next to the person: its body is about 1.2 times the person's height, while a real bottlenose is about 1.4 to 1.6 times. This is minor.",
   "The dolphin looks completely flat in side view. There is no depth or shadow to sit it in the isometric world, though that is acceptable for a swimming sprite."
  ]
 }
}